words. `tears`/`years` fires 21 times over the corpus, 5 in band, on couplets the same pair table records as NOT rhyming (count zero), because cmudict's first pronunciation of `tears` is the rips sense and the table reads `prons[0]`. Neither layer read which sense is on the page. Both fixes were priced in band: a `prons[0]` gate takes 118 to 114 by asserting one convention over another, and an any-pronunciation gate changes nothing at all (136 of 136 listed pairs pass it)","locations":[1],"groups":[],"obligations":[[1,3]],"subject":[]},{"code":"HOMEOTELEUTON","severity":"note","message":"L1/L3 rhyme on the SAME SPELLED ENDING ('rain'/'pain', both -ain) — the laziest class, banned before any frequency judgment","evidence":"spelled rime 'ain' on both sides. The near relations the taxonomy names (declared via Declaration.admit) are the palette that keeps this ban from closing the class: reach for a differently-spelled partner or a declared near rhyme, not the next word in the same spelling family.","locations":[1,3],"groups":["B"],"obligations":[],"subject":[]},{"code":"SCHEME_COLLISION","severity":"note","message":"L2 (A) and L3 (B) RHYME and share no mandated group — 'vein' ~ 'pain' 1.000 ASSONANCE+CONSONANCE+RHYME","evidence":"an unintended rhyme across groups. Whether that is a defect is the writer's call and the loop does not take it: an unmandated rhyme is quite often the best thing in a song, and doctrine 7 makes this a floor rather than a ranking. It is reported because it is the one thing here that IS about the writing","locations":[2,3],"groups":[],"obligations":[],"subject":[]}],"presentation_text":"I left my heart beside the door (the rain)\nYou never came to see the vein\nThe night is cold and full of pain\nAnd nothing grows here anymore\n\n[FINISHED — declared mandate — exit 3 — NO_PROGRESS after 2 round(s) — UNRESOLVED: L1]","shape_status":"declared_line_order","memo_state":"warm","memo_hit":1,"memo_asked":9,"stale_answers":0,"exit":3}

  STANDING AT THE STOP — the findings the open lines and the whole draft still carry, in the report's own spelling:
    L1: FINDING [FLAG] CLICHE_PAIR: 1 rhyme pair(s) on the stock list (lines 1)
         rain/pain. WHAT THIS IS: membership in a hand-typed list of 30 pairs (`FloorDeclaration.cliche_pairs`), nothing more. It fires as a FLAG because the shipped list has a measured interruption rate on held-out human song: author-held out, 200 seeds, 50/50 — the same protocol that gives LEXICAL_MONOTONY 5.43% and FUNCTION_WORD_HEAVY 5.23%. WHERE THAT RATE WAS TAKEN, REPINNED 2026-09-05 (M-239): only in the two band rows, 7.33% at 150-350 tokens (`song`, seed 5th-95th 5.69-8.71%) and 4.51% at 50-150 tokens (`short`, seed 5th-95th 3.96-5.08%) — rows SUPERSEDED on 2026-09-04 for their percentiles, kept for their own drift checks, and still the only measured cliche rates the floor has. This text is at an EXACT STANZA profile (`section`), where the rate is being carried across rather than quoted from a reading taken there. What is known about the two stanza lengths: over the 4,930 corpus items bucketed on 2026-08-14 the check fired on 0.00% of the 86 that landed in `section` exactly and 5.49% of the 437 that landed in `sonnet` exactly — at or under the band rate, which is why the carry-over is not what `sev()` is guarding against. Measuring the rate per length bin over the current lyric range is owed (M-239). WHAT IT IS NOT: a cliche detector, and it does not measure over-familiarity to a living listener. Against this repo's own pair table (`data/song_rhymepair_en.tsv`, 15,409 distinct pairs by author dispersion) the shipped list has low SENSITIVITY: only 4 of the table's top 30 pairs are on it (13.3%; 3 of 10 at the top ten), its own median dispersion rank is #254 of 15,409, and 9 of its 30 pairs never fire anywhere in corpus/song/eng_*. The table's most dispersed rhymes are away/day, be/me, me/thee — none a cliche to anyone now, and none on the list. `quality/relations.py`'s `frequency` Unprovidable and `quality/phrase_commonplace.py` both REFUSE the over-familiarity claim at their own levels, because every admissible English source here is pre-1931; a shorter list does not earn the claim they declined. So this finding says 'this pair is on a named list that interrupts a human songwriter about 6% of the time', and it does not say the rhyme is tired. PRECISION DEFECT, RECORDED NOT FIXED: there is no rhyme test in front of the membership test — it is raw string-set membership on the two end words. `tears`/`years` fires 21 times over the corpus, 5 in band, on couplets the same pair table records as NOT rhyming (count zero), because cmudict's first pronunciation of `tears` is the rips sense and the table reads `prons[0]`. Neither layer read which sense is on the page. Both fixes were priced in band: a `prons[0]` gate takes 118 to 114 by asserting one convention over another, and an any-pronunciation gate changes nothing at all (136 of 136 listed pairs pass it)

